All addresses below are function-relative SASS PCs. Registers are dynamic versions: R12–R43 are reused for partition-input loads after their earlier matrix-multiply uses. Looking only at an earlier definition with the same register number gives a wrong channel map.

## Establishing output channels from weight addresses

At 0x0390, R156 is the first Q output channel of this head pair; R155=R156+16, R154=R156+32, R153=R156+48 at 0x03c0–0x03e0. The final loop load pointers preserve those N16 block coordinates: 0x24d0–0x2500 forms the four block indices, 0x2550–0x25c0 forms pointers, and 0x25f0–0x2660 adds the lane's 16-byte operand offset. R130 is N0, R132 is N16, R134 is N32 and R136 is N48. Offsets 0x80 and 0x18080 select the two K16 halves of a K32 weight tile; they do not change output channels.

For lane t=`lane_id % 4`, one half2 MMA result holds columns `N+2*t` and `N+2*t+1`. The next B register pair in each vector selects N+8. This is the same canonical K16/N16 operand layout described by the adjacent PTX `mma.sync.aligned.m16n8k16.row.col.f16.f16.f16.f16` instructions.

| Logical eight-channel group | Final B vector load | B pair | Final first-row MMA result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Q0 | 0x2630, R120–123 from R130+0x18080 | R120 | 0x2d80, R76–77 |
| Q8 | same | R122 | 0x2d90, R74–75 |
| Q16 | 0x2670, R112–115 from R132+0x18080 | R112 | 0x2da0, R72–73 |
| Q24 | same | R114 | 0x2db0, R70–71 |
| K0 | 0x26a0, R104–107 from R134+0x18080 | R104 | 0x2bb0, R68–69 |
| K8 | same | R106 | 0x2c70, R66–67 |
| K16 | 0x26c0, R96–99 from R136+0x18080 | R96 | 0x2c80, R64–65 |
| K24 | same | R98 | 0x2c90, R62–63 |

The partial-output input loads use matching N0/N16 tile addresses. Q loads at 0x3110 and 0x3220 fill R12–15 and R16–19; the later HADD2 instructions add the matching MMA results. K loads at 0x4dc0 and 0x4ec0 fill R36–39 and R40–43 before matching additions.

## Rounding direction

For Q's first M16 lower row, 0x38b0/0x38d0 add the Q16/Q24 partials to R16/R18; 0x38f0/0x3910 add Q0/Q8 to R12/R14. Then:

```text
3930 HMUL2 R9,  R16, R16       // rounded Q16 square
3950 HMUL2 R70, R18, R18       // rounded Q24 square
3970 HFMA2 R9,  R12, R12, R9  // fuse Q0 square
3990 HFMA2 R70, R14, R14, R70 // fuse Q8 square
39c0 HADD2 R73, R70, R9
```

The upper row is identical at 0x3a00–0x3a50, using R13/R15 and R17/R19. Other Q row pairs repeat the same direction at 0x3aa0–0x3ad0, 0x3b60–0x3bf0, 0x3ba0–0x3c20, 0x3cb0–0x3d10, and 0x3db0–0x3e80.

For K's first lower row, 0x54c0/0x54e0 form K16/K24 in R40/R42; 0x5500/0x5520 form K0/K8 in R36/R38:

```text
5540 HMUL2 R5, R40, R40       // rounded K16 square
5550 HMUL2 R7, R42, R42       // rounded K24 square
5560 HFMA2 R5, R36, R36, R5  // fuse K0 square
5570 HFMA2 R7, R38, R38, R7  // fuse K8 square
5590 HADD2 R46, R7, R5
```

K's other row pairs repeat this direction at 0x55e0–0x5610, 0x5680–0x56b0, 0x5740–0x5770, 0x57d0–0x5850, 0x5930–0x5990, 0x5950–0x59c0, and 0x5ac0–0x5b20.

## Reduction grouping and inverse

Define `f(c)=half_fma(x[c],x[c],half_mul(x[c+16],x[c+16]))`. Each half of a lane first forms `half_add(f(2*t+h),f(8+2*t+h))`, where h selects its low/high halfword. For Q, the following steps are:

| Step | SASS PCs | Logical grouping |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Combine N0/N8 groups | 0x39c0 | channel stride 8 |
| XOR lane 2; add | 0x39f0, 0x3c10 | channel stride 4 |
| XOR lane 1; add | 0x3c40, 0x3df0 | channel stride 2 |
| Swap halfwords; add | 0x3e20, 0x3eb0 | channel stride 1 |
| Half maximum with 0x0410 | 0x3ef0 | floor 6.198883056640625e-5 |
| Half-to-float; reciprocal square root | 0x3f30/0x3f70 and 0x3f80/0x3fd0 | inverse |

K has the same tree at 0x5590, 0x55c0/0x56c0, 0x56f0/0x5880, 0x58c0/0x5900; floor at 0x59f0 and reciprocal square roots at 0x5b10/0x5ba0. K inverse converts back to Half at 0x5c80 before HMUL2 normalization.

A CPU symbolic check compared all four native lane trees with the source stride 8/4/2/1 tree, preserving FMA-versus-multiply structure and allowing commutative swaps only at addition nodes. They match. Native XOR broadcasts can reverse operands at addition nodes relative to lane-zero source evaluation, so this statement is about finite norm sums and grouping, not NaN-payload propagation. The trace does not validate the entire global ABI, checkpoint packing, all FP16 operators, or measured performance.

No active code changes, builds or GPU execution were performed for this review.
